7:## Neurodevelopmental model of schizophrenia revisited: similarity in individual deviation and idiosyncrasy from the normative model of whole-brain white matter tracts and shared brain-cognition covariation with ADHD and ASD
13:The neurodevelopmental model of schizophrenia is supported by multi-level impairments shared among schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders. Despite schizophrenia and typical neurodevelopmental disorders, i.e., autism spectrum disorder (ASD) and attention-de /uniFB01 cit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), as disorders of brain dysconnectivity, no study has ever elucidated whether whole-brain white matter (WM) tracts integrity alterations overlap or diverge between these three disorders. Moreover, whether the linked dimensions of cognition and brain metrics per the Research Domain Criteria framework cut across diagnostic boundaries remains unknown. We aimed to map deviations from normative ranges of whole-brain major WM tracts for individual patients to investigate the similarity and differences among schizophrenia (281 patients subgrouped into the /uniFB01 rst-episode, subchronic and chronic phases), ASD (175 patients), and ADHD (279 patients). Sex-speci /uniFB01 c WM tract normative development was modeled from diffusion spectrum imaging of 626 typically developing controls (5 -40 years). There were three signi /uniFB01 cant /uniFB01 ndings. First, the patterns of deviation and idiosyncrasy of WM tracts were similar between schizophrenia and ADHD alongside ASD, particularly at the earlier stages of schizophrenia relative to chronic stages. Second, using the WM deviation patterns as features, schizophrenia cannot be separated from neurodevelopmental disorders in the unsupervised machine learning algorithm. Lastly, the canonical correlation analysis showed schizophrenia, ADHD, and ASD shared linked cognitive dimensions driven by WM deviations. Together, our results provide new insights into the neurodevelopmental facet of schizophrenia and its brain basis. Individual ' s WM deviations may contribute to diverse arrays of cognitive function along a continuum with phenotypic expressions from typical neurodevelopmental disorders to schizophrenia.
19:Neurobiology underlying schizophrenia is elusive, limiting the development of effective treatment [1]. Among many hypotheses posited, emerging evidence from genetic, transcriptomic, epigenetic, and epidemiological data mainly converges on the neurodevelopmental origins of schizophrenia [2]. This neurodevelopmental model postulates that schizophrenic genetic risk compositions, alongside exogenous environmental input, subtly perturb early brain development. With these developmental insults, psychotic symptoms could develop later in late adolescence/early adulthood when maturational events impose increasing functional demands, leading to decompensation of the neural systems [2, 3]. This model may account for clinical heterogeneity given inter-individual differences in developmental risk factors [2]. Nonetheless, scarce neuroimaging studies have substantiated this heuristic speculation [2 -4].
21:The neurodevelopmental model considers schizophrenia as on a neurodevelopmental continuum [2], supported by the multifaceted relationships of schizophrenia with more typical, early-onset neurodevelopmental disorders, such as autism spectrum disorder (ASD) and attention-de /uniFB01 cit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD). Speci /uniFB01 -cally, both schizophrenia and ASD feature similar impairments in social cognition [5]. A subset of individuals with schizophrenia shows a prior history of ADHD before the onset of psychotic symptoms [6]. ADHD or ASD is diagnosed in many children at risk for schizophrenia [7]. Schizophrenia, ASD, and ADHD also share
27:impairments in numerous domains of executive functions across clinical stages [1, 8, 9]. A prior genome-wide association study indicates signi /uniFB01 cant genetic correlations between schizophrenia and ASD or ADHD, respectively [10]. Moreover, schizophreniaassociated copy number variants show a pleiotropy associated with ADHD and ASD [2, 11]. A resting-state fMRI study [12] also suggests that global brain network organizations are highly similar across ASD, ADHD, and schizophrenia, with subtle differences in local network metrics associated with diagnostic divergence.
29:Nonetheless, some contrasting reports against the close links between schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders exist. For example, a recent study testing structural models of psychopathology at the genomic level suggests ASD and ADHD in the neurodevelopmental factor while schizophrenia in the thoughtproblems factor [13]. A secondary analysis from the ENIGMA consortium [14] has reported that the brain morphology is highly correlated between schizophrenia, major depressive, bipolar, and obsessive-compulsive disorders. Still, those in ASD and ADHD are independent of the common factor aggregating morphological alterations among these disorders. The inconsistency may be explained by the continuing brain and symptom changes at different stages of schizophrenia [1, 3, 4]. But these earlier studies did not consider the illness progression. Moreover, particularly for neuroimaging studies, brain maps of the ' average patient ' inferred from casecontrol differences might not be capable of capturing any particular individual, further resulting in heterogeneity across studies [15]. Therefore, normative modeling [15], combined with strati /uniFB01 cation based on disease stages of schizophrenia, is needed to better model individual variation in brain alterations and explain the relationships between schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders.
31:Based on recent endeavors in schizophrenia [16] and neurodevelopmental disorders [17], the current study leveraged normative modeling to further investigate similarity and distinctness in deviations (to the age/sex norm) of white matter (WM) property, alongside the extent of inter-individual variations ( ' idiosyncrasy ' ), among different stages of schizophrenia, ASD, and ADHD. WM was explicitly targeted because it is the brain structural backbone essential in restraining functional network organization [18] and gray matter coupling [19], given schizophrenia, ASD, and ADHD as disorders of brain dysconnectivity [1, 8, 9]. Patients with schizophrenia show stable patterns of diffuse de /uniFB01 cits in WM connections [20]. The speci /uniFB01 c dysconnectivity may precede symptoms and is associated with cognitive de /uniFB01 cits [21 -30]. Similarly, cumulative evidence supports that individuals with ADHD exhibit distributed patterns of reduced WM connectivity along association and projection pathways, as well as altered microstructural integrity of frontal, striatal, and cerebellar circuits that are critical to behavioral control and motor function [31, 32]. Several altered tracts, such as superior and inferior longitudinal fasciculi, cingulum, and corpus callosum, reported in schizophrenia and ADHD are also impaired in ASD [33 -35]. Moreover, given the knowledge of WM lifespan trajectories, the investigation of WM is proposed as the frontline lore, particularly to reconcile the con /uniFB02 icting evidence in heuristic neurodevelopmental and other (e.g., neurodegenerative) models of schizophrenia [4]. Moving beyond the categorical characterization, the linked dimensions of cognition and brain metrics per the Research Domain Criteria (RDoC) framework [36], cutting across diagnostic boundaries may substantiate the neurodevelopmental facet of schizophrenia. Nonetheless, there has been no report using WM metrics on either categorical collations or dimensional brainbehavior relationships across schizophrenia, ADHD, and ASD.
33:Herein, we developed the normative model of WM microstructural property (generalized fractional anisotropy, GFA), as a function of age, across the lifespan using large-sampled single-site harmonized cross-sectional MRI data from more than 600 typically developing individuals (5 -40 yrs) [17]. This model established the normative ranges of variations in GFA, represented as functions of age and sex. Individual deviations from the norm, alongside variations in individual deviations, were then determined for around 300 individuals with schizophrenia, 300 with ADHD, and 200 with ASD. Further, we delineated linked dimensions of cognition and WM tract /uniFB01 ngerprints, as quanti /uniFB01 ed by the degree of deviation in each tract [36], re /uniFB02 ecting the potential shared neural correlates underlying cognitive impairments across schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders [1, 8, 9]. We hypothesized that the WM pro /uniFB01 les of deviance and idiosyncrasy in schizophrenia, especially in the earlier phase of the illness, would be similar to ASD and ADHD. However, as schizophrenia progressed from the /uniFB01 rst episode to the chronic phase (based on the strati /uniFB01 cation), the level of this similarity among schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders would decrease. We also hypothesized that there would exist different linked dimensions of cognitive function and WM deviation pro /uniFB01 les, cutting cross schizophrenia, and neurodevelopmental disorders.
39:This study recruited 281 patients with schizophrenia (aged 16 -40 yrs), 175 children and adults with ASD (9 -35 yrs), 279 children and adults with ADHD (7 -38 yrs), and 626 typically developing controls (TDC) (Table 1 and STable 1 -2). The three patient groups were recruited from the Department of Psychiatry, National Taiwan University Hospital. The clinical diagnoses were made by senior psychiatrists per both DSM-IV [37] and DSM-5 [38] criteria of schizophrenia, ASD, and ADHD. The diagnosis of ASD was validated by the standard diagnostic assessment of the Autism Diagnostic Interview-Revised [39], while the ADHD diagnosis was con /uniFB01 rmed by the Kiddie Schedule for Affective Disorders and Schizophrenia epidemiological version (K-SADS-E) [40, 41] and modi /uniFB01 ed adult ADHD supplement of the K-SADS-E for ages older than 18 [42, 43]. The schizophrenia cohort included patients in different stages per the duration-based de /uniFB01 nition [44], i.e., ' /uniFB01 rst-episode ' ( /uniFB01 rst diagnosed with duration of illness &lt;1 yr, n = 75, chlorpromazine equivalent dose 211.0 ± 223.6 mg/d), ' subchronic ' (1 -10 yrs of illness, n = 161, 268.6 ± 231.8 mg/d) and ' chronic ' ( ≥ 10 yrs, n = 45, 370.6 ± 256.1 mg/day) (STable 1 -2). All participants had full-scale IQ &gt; 70 based on Wechsler intelligence scores [45, 46]. None of the ASD or ADHD participants had any co-occurring psychotic disorders, and none of the schizophrenia participants had a childhood diagnosis of ASD or ADHD. We recruited TDC from the same community of the patient groups, either from schools or via advertisement, who then received the K-SADS-E or SADS [42, 47] interview to exclude any psychiatric diagnoses (Supplementary Methods).
41:Neurocognitive assessment . Neuropsychological functions were assessed by the Cambridge Neuropsychological Test Automated Battery (CANTAB) [17, 47 -49] (STable 3) and Wechsler intelligence scores [45, 46]. Among CANTAB, One-Touch Stockings of Cambridge, Rapid Visual Information Processing, Reaction Time, Spatial Working Memory, Motor Screening Task, Intra-Extra Dimensional Set Shift, and Paired Associates Learning were assessed. As for IQ pro /uniFB01 les, the subscales on Digit Span (forward, backward), Digit Symbol, Arithmetic, Information, Similarity, Block Design, and Working Memory were selected. (Supplementary Methods).
